  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday through Monday)\nIssued at 200 PM CDT Tue Jun 23 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge hovers near our region over the next several \ndays, promoting a continuation of hazardous heat. Ensembles depict \nan upper trough dipping into the western CONUS Saturday into Sunday, \nshoving the ridge eastward and allowing temperatures to taper down\nby Monday. Until then, many locations are expected to see highs \neach afternoon reaching or exceeding the century mark. Highs \nwithin portions of the Lower Trans Pecos and areas in/near the \nhigher terrain in west Texas are progged to reach the 90s through\nthe extended period. The hottest conditions are expected near and\nalong the river valleys, including much of the Upper Trans Pecos.\nThese areas are progged to generally top out between 105-110 \ndegrees, while a few spots along the Rio Grand in the Big Bend \nreach up to 115 degrees through Sunday. Elsewhere, afternoon \ntemperatures Thursday through Sunday will range between 100-105 \ndegrees. Triple digit heat becomes less expansive Monday as \nheights lower aloft given the proximity of the upper trough. \nOvernight lows largely fall to the 70s each night. \n\nAside from hot conditions, winds will be breezy most afternoons and \nevenings. This as nearly persistent lee troughing over the Plains \nproduces breezy south/southeasterly winds within the eastern half of \nour region. Moist upslope flow combined with disturbances aloft and \ndaytime heating brings low (10-30%) rain chances to portions of\nthe northern Permian Basin and areas west of the Pecos River most\nafternoons. The best PoPs will tend to favor locations in/near \nthe Davis Mountains. Rain chances look to increase further Monday \nafternoon and evening, as the aforementioned trough gains more \ninfluence over our weather pattern.",
